.TH GLOWTERM 1 "2026-11-16" "glowterm 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
glowterm \- run a shell with an animation behind its text
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B glowterm
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RB [ \-\-
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B glowterm
runs a program, by default your login shell, inside the current terminal and
draws an animation in the parts of the screen the program has left empty.
Text is never covered: the animation shows only in blank cells, and is dimmed
behind lines of text so that they stay readable.
.PP
The program runs in a pseudo-terminal.
.B glowterm
keeps its own model of the screen, mixes the animation into it, and redraws
the real terminal about twenty times a second.
It ends when the program ends, restores the terminal to the state it found it
in, and exits with the program's exit status.
.PP
With no
.IR COMMAND ,
.B glowterm
runs
.B $SHELL \-l
(or
.B /bin/zsh \-l
if
.B SHELL
is not set) in the current directory.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-anim " " \fINAME\fR
Start with the animation
.IR NAME .
The default is
.BR matrix .
See
.B ANIMATIONS
below.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List the animations and exit.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-intensity " " \fIN\fR
Overall brightness of the animation, from 0.0 to 1.0.
The default is 1.0.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-text\-dim " " \fIN\fR
Share of the animation's brightness that is kept behind lines of text, from
0.0 to 1.0.
The default is 0.3.
A value of 0 removes the animation from text lines altogether.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-density " " \fIN\fR
How busy the animation is.
The default is 1.0.
Each animation interprets this in its own way: more rain, more stars, taller
flames and so on.
.B arcade
ignores it.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-fps " " \fIN\fR
Animation frames drawn per second, from 1 to 60.
The default is 20.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-scroll " " \fIN\fR
Lines scrolled for each notch of the mouse wheel, from 1 to 100.
The default is 3.
.TP
.BR \-\-scrollback " " \fIN\fR
Lines of history to keep.
The default is 10000.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-mouse
Do not capture the mouse.
Click-and-drag text selection then works as usual in the terminal, but the
wheel cannot scroll through history.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-hotkey
Pass Ctrl+] to the program instead of using it to switch animation.
.TP
.B \-\-256
Use the 256-colour palette even if the terminal supports 24-bit colour.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of options and exit.
.SH ANIMATIONS
.TP
.B matrix
Katakana digital rain.
.TP
.B aurora
Slow curtains of green and violet light.
.TP
.B fire
Flames rising from the bottom edge.
.TP
.B starfield
Stars streaming out from the centre.
.TP
.B plasma
Flowing full-spectrum colour fields.
.TP
.B cyberspace
A flight through a neon city of data towers.
.TP
.B nightride
Tail-light trails through a neon city at night.
.TP
.B arcade
Original early-console-style games playing themselves.
.SH KEYS AND MOUSE
.TP
.B Ctrl+]
Switch to the next animation, in the order listed above.
Its name is shown briefly in the top-right corner.
.TP
.B Mouse wheel
Scroll back and forward through earlier output.
While scrolled back, a marker in the top-right corner shows the position, the
cursor is hidden, and new output does not move the view.
In a full-screen program that has not asked for the mouse itself, such as
.BR less (1),
the wheel sends arrow keys instead.
.TP
.B Typing
Returns the view to the live screen.
.PP
Because
.B glowterm
captures the mouse, selecting text requires the terminal's override key to be
held while dragging (Option in iTerm2, Shift in many other terminals), unless
.B \-\-no\-mouse
is given.
.PP
All other input is passed to the program unchanged.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SHELL
The program to run when no
.I COMMAND
is given.
.TP
.B COLORTERM
If this contains
.B truecolor
or
.BR 24bit ,
24-bit colour is used; otherwise colours are mapped to the 256-colour palette.
.TP
.B TERM
Set to
.B xterm\-256color
for the program that
.B glowterm
runs, whatever it was outside.
.TP
.B GLOWTERM
Set to 1 for the program that
.B glowterm
runs.
.B glowterm
refuses to start if it is already set, to prevent running inside itself.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status of the program that was run.
1 if
.B glowterm
could not start (for example, an unknown option, or input or output is not a
terminal).
143 if
.B glowterm
was ended by a hangup, interrupt, quit or termination signal sent from
outside.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run the login shell over the default animation:
.PP
.RS
.B glowterm
.RE
.PP
Start with the aurora, dimmer, and with nothing behind lines of text:
.PP
.RS
.B glowterm \-a aurora \-i 0.6 \-t 0
.RE
.PP
Watch
.BR htop (1)
over a starfield:
.PP
.RS
.B glowterm \-a starfield \-\- htop
.RE
.SH NOTES
The terminal's own scrollback does not apply inside
.BR glowterm ;
use the mouse wheel as described above.
.PP
Terminal emulation is provided by a library and is simpler than that of a
full terminal.
Inline images, hyperlinks, extended keyboard protocols and the line-drawing
character set are not supported; a few programs' borders therefore appear as
letters.
.PP
A
.BR tmux (1)
server started from inside
.B glowterm
inherits the
.B GLOWTERM
variable, so its panes will refuse to run
.B glowterm
until the variable is unset.
.PP
If the terminal is ever left in an unusable state, run
.BR reset (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1),
.BR script (1),
.BR reset (1)
